    "case_summary": "A 67-year-old female presented with three-year history of worsening dysarthria and dysphagia, choking episodes provoked when lying supine, a one-year history of constant pain at right occipital and cervical paraspinal areas and a 20-pound weight loss. She reported a distant one-year history of smoking with no alcohol or illicit drug use.\n\nThere was right tongue deviation, atrophy of the right side of her tongue, limited tongue movements and difficulty with tongue protrusion, but no tongue fasciculation. She had lingual and palatal dysarthria, and slow, but symmetric palate elevation. The rest of her neurological exam, including muscle and facial strength and reflexes, was unremarkable.\n\n- Laboratory tests: Acetylcholine receptor antibodies, TSH, B12, ANA, ESR, and CK were normal.- EMG findings: Active denervation changes in the left lower lumbar paraspinals and medial gastrocnemius muscle, and minimal active denervation in the left first dorsal interosseous and pronator teres.- Imaging Studies:  1. MRI of the nasopharynx     Image Description: Shows infiltrative process at the base and root of the tongue, invading the most inferior aspects of the mouth floor, including the lingual neurovascular bundles    2. Maxillofacial CT     Image Description: Shows an enhancing, infiltrative, and partially necrotic mass of the floor of her mouth, centered on the left side and involving the midline, measuring 17 × 35 × 21 mm. Infiltration of the intrinsic tongue muscles with tongue atrophy.    3. Flexible laryngoscopy: Shows base of tongue fullness with prominent lymphatic tissue  - Pathology:  Biopsy results: Focal, non-keratinizing invasive squamous cell carcinoma  Additional findings: P16– INK and Cyto5/6 immunohistochemistry were positive",
